        task2: {
          title: "Does international travel broaden the mind?",
          text: "Some people believe that international travel broadens the mind, while others argue that tourists learn very little about the countries they visit.\n\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.\n\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\n\nWrite at least 250 words.",
          sample: `It is often claimed that travelling abroad makes people more open-minded, whereas others insist that most tourists return home having learned almost nothing. Both views contain some truth, and the reality, in my opinion, depends largely on how a person chooses to travel.

On the one hand, international travel can genuinely broaden the mind. When travellers spend time in another country, they encounter different customs, foods, beliefs and ways of organising daily life. A visitor to Japan, for example, may be struck by the politeness and order of public life, while a trip to a village in Africa can challenge comfortable assumptions about wealth and happiness. Such experiences are difficult to gain from books or films, and they often make people more tolerant and curious on their return.

On the other hand, critics point out that much modern tourism keeps visitors inside a comfortable bubble. Tourists who stay in large beach resorts, eat familiar food and speak only to other tourists may spend two weeks abroad without a single real conversation with a local person. Package tours can turn famous cities into little more than backgrounds for photographs. In these cases, travel confirms existing opinions rather than challenging them, and little genuine learning takes place.

In my view, travel itself is neither educational nor useless; its value depends on the traveller's attitude. Those who learn a few words of the local language, use public transport, visit ordinary neighbourhoods and ask questions will almost certainly come home with a wider understanding of the world. Those who do not make this effort will not.

In conclusion, international travel has the potential to broaden the mind, but it does not do so automatically. The destination matters far less than the curiosity and openness that the traveller brings to it.`
        }
