        task2: {
          title: "Fighting poverty: money or education?",
          text: "Some people think that the best way to reduce poverty is to give poor people free money, while others believe that education and job training are more effective.\n\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.\n\nGive reasons for your answer and include any relevant examples from your own knowledge or experience.\n\nWrite at least 250 words.",
          sample: `Poverty remains one of the world's greatest problems, and people disagree strongly about the best solution. Some argue that governments should simply give money to the poor, while others believe that education and training offer a better long-term answer.

Those who support direct financial help point out that poverty is, at its simplest, a lack of money. A family that cannot afford food, medicine or school fees needs help immediately, not in ten years' time. Small regular payments, such as child benefits, allow parents to make their own decisions and have been shown to improve children's health and school attendance. Supporters also argue that this approach is honest and direct: instead of spending money on large government programmes, the state gives it straight to the people who need it.

On the other hand, many people believe that free money treats only the symptoms of poverty, not its causes. If a person has no skills and no job, the payments must continue forever, which is expensive for the state and can, some claim, discourage people from working. Education and job training, by contrast, give individuals the means to earn their own living permanently. A young woman who learns computer skills, for example, can support herself and her family for the rest of her life, and her children are far less likely to grow up poor.

In my opinion, the two approaches are not real alternatives; they work best together. Financial support is essential as a safety net, because nobody can study or train while their family is hungry. However, education should be the main long-term strategy, since it changes lives rather than simply maintaining them.

In conclusion, giving money to the poor provides vital short-term relief, but education and training are the more powerful tools for ending poverty permanently. Governments should fund both, with the greater emphasis on education.`
        }
