          sample: `The chart compares how people used their free time in 2000 and in 2020, showing the percentage of leisure time spent on five activities.

Overall, watching television was the most popular pastime in 2000 but fell considerably by 2020, while using the internet grew from the smallest activity to the largest. Playing sport also became more popular, whereas reading declined sharply.

In 2000, television accounted for 35 per cent of free time, the biggest share by far. Playing sport was second, at 18 per cent, followed closely by reading at 16 per cent. The internet took up only 4 per cent of leisure time, with other activities making up the remaining 27 per cent.

Twenty years later, the picture had changed dramatically. The internet had risen to 31 per cent, overtaking television, which dropped to 22 per cent. Sport increased to 24 per cent, becoming the second most popular activity. In contrast, reading fell to just 9 per cent, and the share of other activities decreased to 14 per cent. Notably, sport was the only one of the three most popular activities in 2000 to increase its share over the period.`
